    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97266.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Well, good morning, everyone, or almost good afternoon. We're really honored today to have with us Congressmen Randy Holgren, hailing from Plano, Illinois, where he represents the 14th District of Illinois. Now, I don't think too many of you have made that trip out US 34 into Plano, but actually, if you saw the movie about six years ago called The Man of Steel, Smallville, is actually Plano, Illinois, and the Kent Family Farm on their quarter section is a little more than a mile outside of town. So you've actually been to Plano, or if you haven't get the movie Netflix and check it out. Congressman Holkin was first elected to Congress in 2010 and serves on the House Financial Services Science Space and Technology Committees. He also serves as the vice president, vice chairman, excuse me, of the capital market subcommittee. And in that role, he has an integral insight into the oversight of the US financial services industry and its regulations. Congressman Holkren has been at the center of legislative action in financial services issues, currently very engaged in a somewhat esoteric but very important to this industry issue related to having the appropriate level of risk-based bank capital rules. Those are having, as I think we've discussed a number of times earlier in the conference, a negative impact on particularly exchange-created derivatives markets. And this is a topic of significant interest to all of us. I also like to point out that Congressman Holgren's son, Cole, is with us today. Cole, stand up and wave to everybody. Tomorrow, Cinco de Mayo, is Cole's birthday. So I hope everybody who sees him in the hall will wish him a happy birthday. Congressman Helkwin is actually one of us. He worked in the financial services industry prior to being elected to Congress in 2010. And he has those wonderful credentials the number of us have struggled through. The FINRA Series 7 general securities Series 6 investment management and 63 blue sky or multi-state licenses. I didn't ask him backstage whether he was still current on his continuing education requirements, but I don't think House of Representatives is an appropriate broker dealer,",
