  {
    "episode_id": 97266,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97266.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "I don't know. It's a good question. I wasn't sure if we'd actually get tax reform done as we're going into it. If you were to ask me a year and a half ago, I thought there was maybe a 25% chance that we'd get it done. And then it happened. And so I was really encouraged. I think it was a good process. I think it was an open process allowing a lot of voices to be heard. And I think, as you said, John, By and large, it's very good as I travel around my district. 90% plus of people are seeing benefits from it. And even more than that, with the growing economy, I think tax reform is important. Let's have a tax code that doesn't punish productivity but encourages it. I think we've taken some steps. But even then, it's not perfect. There's still work that needs to be done. What I think would be likely is more tinkering, some refinement of some things that maybe got missed. One area that I focus pretty heavily on is municipal finance. I'm co-chair of our municipal finance caucus and just recognizing again how important municipal bonds are in the tax treatment of municipal bonds and also private activity bonds which I think are the most poorly named instruments in history. I'm doing everything I can to change it to public good bonds. Because private activity bonds sounds like someone's getting a special deal here when in reality it's how hospitals are built. University of Chicago, their entire medical facility was built using private activity bonds. The intermodal transportation system in Joliet that employs thousands and thousands of people was private activity bonds. A great public good. But anyhow, we were able to protect that. I'm grateful that it remained in their work with Dutch Rupersberger, Democrat from Maryland, TNR, Co-Chairman. And we put together about 160 some of our colleagues who signed a letter to Chairman Brady and our leadership of the importance of municipal finance. So we were able to protect that. One thing that did get taken away was advanced refunding, which is an important tool in the bond industry and more importantly for local communities to be able to see opportunity to make some changes there. So that's one area that I would love to see us go back and address. There's other things that I think are clear issues that got missed. My hope is we'll be able to do it. But as you said, John, the window closes quickly before elections. And I would say maybe even quicker than usual this year because of just how heightened these elections are. We've seen with some of the special elections, we've seen with the number of people who are retiring and open seats that are in Congress. It is going to be a really tough November. And so I think if we don't get something done in the next couple months, It's going to be tough last minute. The one thing that maybe could change would be some tinkering on tax reform and maybe infrastructure could be something that could bring both sides together. I would love that. I think that would be a good thing for our nation.",
