    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97266.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "That's a great question. I don't think they rely on me but I still talk to them and I try and push my ideas so my hope is that I have some influence and can maybe direct some of the discussion and some of the prioritization. I think that's the challenge with the volume of legislation and information in each committee. It's picking priorities. It's what's going to rise to the top, what's going to get the attention. Four time is the ultimate challenge for us and even more so in the Senate. So we can move quite a bit. We've moved over 400 bills through the House in the last year and a half, but only a handful of those have moved through the Senate. So that's always our calculus too is let's have discussion, let's have debate, let's make sure that we're getting important issues out there. and to have them heard and debated. And best as we can, let's ultimately get things moving into law, which is what we want to see happen. But, you know, figuring out the Senate is way above my pay grade of trying to figure out what moves them, what gets them active. And, you know, so that is one specific thing that we have been working on. We passed significant Dodd-Frank reform last year. The Choice Act, a lot of good stuff in there. We've gone since then just to do individual smaller packages that we've passed out of the house. Almost all of them, the smaller packages, almost always have been bipartisan. And in some cases, unanimous going through committee, which is incredible. And so I'm encouraged by that. But my hope is, you know, the Senate was able to act. It wasn't as much as I would like to have seen done in their regulatory reform package that they passed out. Bipartisan, you know, I think it was 15 or 17 Democrats that supported that. That's great. So my hope is what we've been trying to weigh is, is there anything more we can get? Does it make sense to hold back any longer to try and get more from them? What we're hearing is, My sense is we're going to move forward. We're going to take and approve the Senate bill 2155. It's going to pass in the House. I think it will. I think it'll pass in the next couple of weeks. I think we'll pass that on to the president. The president will sign it. The reality is it's having real cost on real people in real companies the longer we wait. And I think What we're getting in response back from the Senate is get this done and then we have hope of maybe another package, maybe a little bit smaller, a little bit more targeted, again, of these things that have gotten 45, 50, 55, 60 votes out of financial services committee. So unanimous or close to unanimous Republican and Democratic support through committee. going on to the floor and getting 350 to 400 votes in the House. Those are the types of things that I think the Senate maybe still could take up in a separate bill. But let's not do anything that allows certain folks, maybe especially from the northeast, who would love to kill all of this. And let's get some good things done. Let's get some real Dodd-Frank reform through, regulatory reform through, and hopefully build some momentum to get more things done.",
