    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97266.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "Well, yes, I hope so, and I'm encouraged. So this is, it takes a little bit of time to talk through, to tell my colleagues or their staff to help them understand why this is important, why this is something we need to take action on, or push our regulators to take action if they can themselves. So my hope is, one or the other both happen, that we've got legislation and one of the good things of a week like this has been a district work week for us. So I've been back in my district all over the district. But our staff for the most part are still out in Washington, D.C. And so Bill Hulse, my financial advisor, who does our financial services work, has been working with a lot of other offices, democratic offices especially, to help them understand we're getting a very positive response back. We were hoping that we were going to have a markup this coming week and that this potentially could be part of that. Just got word literally before I walked in that we're not going to do the markup this week. So I'm disappointed. I don't know what happened. It's not put off indefinitely. I think it's put off for another week, which in some ways is maybe good for us. It gives us a little bit more time when I get back on Monday to be working with colleagues to get them signed up on it. But I'm hopeful that we can get this done, pass through the financial services committee, pass through the House. Not sure, again, if this is something the Senate would pick up. But oftentimes it takes our activity having legislation that is there in written form that we can give to them that we can say, hey, we're acting on this. That kind of wakes up the regulators to say it's not good enough for us to wait for the Basel committees. What I'm hearing is it could be two, three, four years before",
